+# Hegel property-based testing report for `ipnet`
+
+Hegel (`hegeltest` 0.28.2) property tests were added to the existing unit-test
+modules in `src/ipnet.rs` (15 tests) and `src/mask.rs` (2 tests). Three tests
+fail; they pin two genuine library bugs and are deliberately left failing (see
+**Suspected bugs**).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All properties are grounded in the crate's rustdoc contracts, doc examples, or
+independent mathematical facts about CIDR blocks. Evidence noted per property.
+
+In `src/ipnet.rs::tests`:
+
+1. **`hegel_ipv4net_display_parse_roundtrip`** — `s.parse::<Ipv4Net>() == Ok(net)`
+   for `s = net.to_string()`, all prefix lengths 0–32, arbitrary (boundary-boosted)
+   addresses. Evidence: `Display` writes `"{addr}/{prefix}"`; `FromStr` documented
+   to parse CIDR notation (RFC 4632).
+2. **`hegel_ipv6net_display_parse_roundtrip`** — same for `Ipv6Net`, prefix 0–128,
+   exercising the custom IPv6 parser in `src/parser.rs` (zero compression,
+   IPv4-mapped forms).
+3. **`hegel_ipnet_display_parse_roundtrip`** — same for the `IpNet` enum
+   (exercises the `read_or` v4-then-v6 dispatch path).
+4. **`hegel_from_str_never_panics`** — `FromStr` for all three types returns
+   `Err`, never panics, on arbitrary unicode text, near-miss CIDR-alphabet
+   strings, and valid displays. Evidence: `FromStr` returns `Result`; parser
+   calls `Ipv4Net::new(..).unwrap()` internally, so this checks the parser's
+   prefix bound (`read_number(10, 2, 33)`) really protects that unwrap.
+5. **`hegel_ipnet_parse_agrees_with_concrete_types`** — `IpNet::from_str`
+   succeeds iff `Ipv4Net::from_str` or `Ipv6Net::from_str` succeeds, with V4
+   taking precedence and equal values. Evidence: `Parser::read_ip_net` tries v4
+   then v6.
+6. **`hegel_netmask_hostmask_are_complementary`** — `netmask ^ hostmask == !0`,
+   `netmask.leading_ones() == prefix_len == netmask.count_ones()`, and neither
+   changes IP family. Evidence: netmask/hostmask doc examples; definition of a
+   prefix mask.
+7. **`hegel_ipv4_hosts_count_and_endpoints_match_docs`** — host count is
+   `2^(32-p) - 2` for `p < 31` (network/broadcast excluded, per `hosts()` docs),
+   `2` for `/31`, `1` for `/32`; first/last hosts match the documented endpoints.
+   Checked for **all** prefix lengths including 0 (the `count()` override is O(1)).
+8. **`hegel_ipv6_hosts_count_and_endpoints_match_docs`** — IPv6 `hosts()` spans
+   `network()..=broadcast()` (docs: no exclusion for IPv6); count is `2^(128-p)`
+   for `p >= 65` (bound protects the test from the *documented* count overflow,
+   not the library's contract).
+9. **`hegel_contains_addr_matches_prefix_bits_oracle`** — `net.contains(&addr)`
+   agrees with an independent oracle: the first `prefix_len` bits of `addr` and
+   `net.addr()` agree; cross-family containment is `false` (documented). Draws
+   are boosted so ~half the addresses are snapped inside the net.
+10. **`hegel_contains_net_iff_longer_prefix_and_network_inside`** — CIDR fact:
+    `a.contains(&b)` iff `b.prefix_len() >= a.prefix_len()` and `b.network()`
+    lies in `a` (checked with the bit oracle). v4 and v6.
+11. **`hegel_supernet_is_unique_immediate_parent`** — `supernet()` is `None` iff
+    `prefix_len == 0`; otherwise it is truncated, has `prefix_len - 1`, and
+    contains the net — which uniquely determines it. Evidence: `supernet()` docs.
+12. **`hegel_subnets_partition_their_network`** — `net.subnets(np)` yields exactly
+    `2^(np-p)` subnets of prefix `np` that tile `[network(), broadcast()]`
+    contiguously in order; `np < p` and `np > max` return `Err(PrefixLenError)`.
+    Evidence: `subnets()` docs and examples. (Prefix delta capped at 8 to bound
+    test time, not a library limit.)
+13. **`hegel_ipv4_subnets_iterator_covers_exact_range`** — `Ipv4Subnets::new(start,
+    end, min_prefix_len)` yields subnets with `prefix_len >= min_prefix_len` that
+    tile exactly `[start, end]` (empty when `start > end`). Evidence: `Ipv4Subnets`
+    doc: "Generates the subnets between the provided `start` and `end` IP
+    addresses inclusive of `end`... using a prefix length not less than
+    `min_prefix_len`." **FAILS — real bug, see below.**
+14. **`hegel_ipv6_subnets_iterator_covers_exact_range`** — IPv6 analog.
+    **FAILS — real bug, see below.**
+15. **`hegel_aggregate_preserves_address_membership`** — `IpNet::aggregate`
+    returns truncated nets covering exactly the same address set as the inputs;
+    membership probed at arbitrary addresses plus every input's boundary and
+    one-past-boundary addresses. Evidence: `aggregate()` docs/examples merge
+    without changing coverage; `test_aggregate_issue44` shows exact-coverage
+    intent. **FAILS — real bug, see below.**
+16. **`hegel_ord_consistent_with_contains`** — for truncated nets, a strict
+    superset sorts strictly before its subsets (`Ord` derived on
+    `(addr, prefix_len)`); structural consistency between ordering and
+    containment.
+17. **`hegel_trunc_preserves_range_and_is_idempotent`** — `trunc()` keeps
+    prefix/network/broadcast, sets `addr == network`, and is idempotent.
+    Evidence: `trunc()` docs.
+
+In `src/mask.rs::tests`:
+
+18. **`hegel_prefix_netmask_roundtrip`** — a mask built independently
+    (`!0 << (bits - p)`) converts back via `ipv{4,6}_mask_to_prefix` to `Ok(p)`,
+    and `with_netmask(addr, mask) == new(addr, p)`. Evidence: mask function docs
+    and `with_netmask` examples.
+19. **`hegel_mask_to_prefix_accepts_exactly_contiguous_masks`** — an arbitrary
+    u32/u128 is accepted iff it is a contiguous run of ones then zeros
+    (independent check: `leading_ones() + trailing_zeros() == bits`), and the
+    returned prefix equals `leading_ones()`. Evidence: "If the mask is invalid
+    this will return an `PrefixLenError`."
+
+Supporting generators (in `src/ipnet.rs::tests`): boundary-boosted address bits
+(`0`, `1`, `MAX-1`, `MAX` mixed with the full range), boundary-boosted prefix
+lengths, net-inside-net constructors, and a CIDR-ish string generator.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test`: **49 passed, 3 failed** (plus 56 doc-tests passing when run
+  separately). The three failures are deliberate — they pin real library bugs
+  (below) and are marked `KNOWN BUG` in the source.
+- Liveness check performed per the skill: temporarily inverting the v4
+  roundtrip assertion produced a shrunk minimal counterexample (`0.0.0.0/0`),
+  then was reverted and the `.hegel/` database removed.
+- Exploratory high-count run performed per the skill: at the default 100 cases
+  the whole suite initially passed; at `test_cases = 1000` the three failures
+  appeared. The failing tests keep `test_cases = 1000` (with boundary-boosted
+  generators) so the failures reproduce reliably; verified on repeated runs
+  from a clean `.hegel/` state — same three tests fail every time, all others
+  pass every time.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+Both bugs were confirmed with standalone (non-hegel) reproducers before being
+attributed to the library. Tests kept failing as instructed.
+
+### Bug 1: `Ipv4Subnets`/`Ipv6Subnets` emit subnets outside the requested range when the range is one address short of the full space
+
+- **Minimal counterexamples** (shrunk by hegel, confirmed standalone):
+  - `Ipv4Subnets::new("0.0.0.0", "255.255.255.254", 0)` yields `[0.0.0.0/0]`,
+    which contains `255.255.255.255` — one past the inclusive `end`.
+  - `Ipv4Subnets::new("0.0.0.1", "255.255.255.255", 0)` yields `[0.0.0.1/0]` — a
+    non-truncated net whose network is `0.0.0.0`, i.e. it covers an address
+    *before* `start`.
+  - IPv6 analog: `Ipv6Subnets::new("::", "ffff:...:fffe", 0)` yields `[::/0]`.
+- **Why it's a library bug**: the type's documentation says it "Generates the
+  subnets between the provided `start` and `end` IP addresses inclusive of
+  `end`". Emitting a subnet containing addresses outside `[start, end]`
+  violates that contract.
+- **Code path**: `next_ipv4_subnet` (src/ipnet.rs:1612) computes
+  `let range = end.saturating_sub(start).saturating_add(1);` and special-cases
+  `range == core::u32::MAX && min_prefix_len == 0` to return `start/0`. The
+  saturated `range` cannot distinguish a true range of `2^32` (full space,
+  where `/0` is correct) from `2^32 - 1` (one short, where it is not).
+  `next_ipv6_subnet` (src/ipnet.rs:1626) has the identical flaw with `u128`.
+- **Pinned by**: `hegel_ipv4_subnets_iterator_covers_exact_range`,
+  `hegel_ipv6_subnets_iterator_covers_exact_range`.
+
+### Bug 2: `aggregate` adds an address when an input's broadcast is one below the top of the address space
+
+- **Minimal counterexamples** (shrunk by hegel, confirmed standalone):
+  - `Ipv4Net::aggregate(&vec!["255.255.255.254/32"])` returns
+    `["255.255.255.254/31"]`, which contains `255.255.255.255` — an address in
+    no input network.
+  - `Ipv6Net::aggregate(&vec!["ffff:...:fffe/128"])` returns
+    `["ffff:...:fffe/127"]`.
+- **Why it's a library bug**: aggregation is documented (and demonstrated in
+  `test_aggregate` / `test_aggregate_issue44`) as merging networks; it must not
+  change the covered address set. Returning a strict superset breaks any
+  ACL/routing use of the result.
+- **Code path**: `Ipv4Net::interval()` (src/ipnet.rs:927) represents a net as a
+  half-open interval `(network, broadcast.saturating_add(1))`. For a net ending
+  at `255.255.255.254` the exclusive end is `u32::MAX` — the same value the
+  saturation produces for nets ending at `255.255.255.255`. `aggregate`
+  (src/ipnet.rs:952) then does `if end != core::u32::MAX { end =
+  end.saturating_sub(1) }`, assuming `end == MAX` always means "covers through
+  MAX", and hands the widened range to `Ipv4Subnets`. Same logic for IPv6 at
+  src/ipnet.rs:1303/1328. (Inputs that exactly tile `[0.0.0.1,
+  255.255.255.255]` additionally reach Bug 1 through the same call.)
+- **Pinned by**: `hegel_aggregate_preserves_address_membership`.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None. No property had to be withdrawn as asserting something the crate never
+promised. Two generator constraints were added for test-resource reasons only
+(both commented in the code as such, per the skill's generator discipline):
+the subnet-count cap (prefix delta ≤ 8, range/min-prefix scenarios in the
+`Subnets` tests) and the IPv6 `hosts().count()` check being limited to
+`prefix >= 65` because `count()` is *documented* to overflow/panic on ranges
+that exceed the counter.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+The skill and Rust reference worked well end to end; the three failures found
+are real, previously-unpinned bugs. Specific notes:
+
+- **The `no_std` gotcha in the Rust reference was load-bearing.** The first
+  compile failed with E0599 (`to_string` on `&'static str` inside
+  `#[hegel::test]`'s expansion) and E0425 (`String` not in scope), exactly as
+  the reference's Setup section predicts; `use alloc::string::{String,
+  ToString};` in each test module fixed it. One small addition would help: the
+  reference mentions the generated code's `.to_string()` call, but a
+  `#[hegel::composite]` returning `String` also needs `String` itself in scope
+  (E0425), which is easy to misread as a different problem.
+- **The boundary-conjunction advice found the bugs.** All three failures need
+  multiple simultaneous boundary draws (e.g. `start == 0`, `end == MAX - 1`,
+  `min_prefix_len == 0`), which never coincided in a default 100-case run. The
+  skill's two recommendations — boost boundaries with `one_of!`/`sampled_from`,
+  and do one exploratory ~10x run — were exactly what surfaced them.
+- **Gap: no guidance for keeping a probabilistic real-bug failure failing.**
+  The skill says to keep the test as written when a real bug is found, but at
+  the default case count these failures reproduce only ~20% of runs (and the
+  `.hegel/` replay database is per-machine and gitignored). I resolved it by
+  boosting the implicated conjunction in the generator and raising
+  `test_cases` on just the pinning tests, with comments. The skill could bless
+  a pattern for this ("make the pinning test fail deterministically:
+  targeted boost + elevated case count, or a fixed seed").
+- **Gap follow-up to gotcha 13 (no global case-count override):** doing the
+  10x pass over 17 tests means editing every attribute; a `sed` round-trip
+  works but also rewrites comments that mention `#[hegel::test]`. A
+  documented env-var or a suggested one-liner would reduce friction.
+- Everything else matched the reference exactly: `cargo add --dev hegeltest`,
+  the `hegel` crate-name split, `one_of!`, `sampled_from`, composites,
+  `.map()` on a composite generator, plain `&TestCase` helper functions, and
+  counterexample output (`draw_N` for helper-internal draws) all behaved as
+  documented.
